WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.094
Containers often receive configuration through environment variables, mounted files, orchestration settings, or external configuration services.

2
00:00:10.094 --> 00:00:17.004
Secrets may include API keys, database passwords, certificates, tokens, and application credentials.

3
00:00:17.004 --> 00:00:22.128
The central rule is simple: secrets should not be baked into container images.

4
00:00:22.128 --> 00:00:29.892
Images may be stored, copied, cached, scanned, shared, or reused long after the original deployment context changes.

5
00:00:29.892 --> 00:00:33.930
Secret delivery should be separate from image construction.

6
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:43.635
Runtime secret mounts, managed secret stores, short-lived credentials, and rotation workflows are more durable patterns than hardcoded values.

7
00:00:43.635 --> 00:00:47.828
Secret rotation concepts should be planned before an incident.

8
00:00:47.828 --> 00:00:59.164
If a key leaks, teams need to know which workloads use it, how to replace it, how to restart or reload affected containers, and how to verify that old credentials no longer work.

9
00:00:59.164 --> 00:01:01.959
Logs are another data protection concern.

10
00:01:01.959 --> 00:01:12.984
Applications can accidentally write passwords, tokens, request bodies, connection strings, or sensitive customer data to stdout, stderr, or sidecar logging paths.

11
00:01:12.984 --> 00:01:21.913
Container platforms make log collection convenient, which means sensitive data can spread quickly into centralized logging systems.

12
00:01:21.913 --> 00:01:28.823
Logging should preserve operational value without turning every log pipeline into a secret repository.

13
00:01:28.823 --> 00:01:31.308
Persistent data needs its own design.

14
00:01:31.308 --> 00:01:37.442
Volumes, backups, snapshots, and database connections may outlive individual containers.

15
00:01:37.442 --> 00:01:44.585
Encryption at rest and in transit, at a high level, protects data when stored and when moving across networks.

16
00:01:44.585 --> 00:01:56.153
Configuration should be separated from images so the same artifact can move between environments safely while environment-specific secrets and settings remain controlled.
